A||||12||gangrenefiero||3.4 vs 3.8; Pros and Cons ||||1||
Z||000000||gangrenefiero||09-29-2001||12:30 AM||||Im looking for a new engine in my fiero. But im getting mixed signals about which engine would be the best one to get. I was looking at the 3.4 from the new grand am GT and the 3.8 NA or SC like my brothers "Lil Devil" and "Bills NOS 3800". I cant tell if its worth the little more price. What about the difficulty of installation and any other hidden pop ups that come about in the middle of installation. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||09-29-2001||06:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gangrenefiero:<BR><B>Im looking for a new engine in my fiero. But im getting mixed signals about which engine would be the best one to get. I was looking at the 3.4 from the new grand am GT and the 3.8 NA or SC like my brothers "Lil Devil" and "Bills NOS 3800". I cant tell if its worth the little more price. What about the difficulty of installation and any other hidden pop ups that come about in the middle of installation. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Each will have their particular problems pop up, so you have to deal with that as they occur...<BR>What added expence are you talking about? Are you buying the engines from a bone yard, or fresh overhauls?<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||09-29-2001||07:02 AM||||You can get a new 3.4 crate engine that will bolt onto the Fiero, the only modification would be repositioning the starter.  It can use the Fiero ECM and other components.<P>The 3800 will need the wiring harness, new ECM.  The electrical wires in front of the firewall will probably require modifications if you want a/c etc things to work.<P>While I had my 3.4 professionally installed, the master was also doing a 3800 s/c install and I heard a lot of cursing about the wiring problems.<P>I suspect that there may be more potential for the hidden "pop ups" with the 3800.  Granted the 3800 will have more power.  But where can you safely drive 120mph+ for long periods of time.  Lighting up tires and smoking them is lots of fun, tires are money.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||09-29-2001||07:58 AM||||didn't notice if they've said in their posts, but did your brothers do their own? i'm not sure if any of my brothers could change their own oil. if they did i'd go with a 3800 just because you have two people who have experience with it.<P>plus if they don't help you, you can tell momma on 'em. <P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000004||westtexas||09-29-2001||08:48 AM||||Old Lar, I'm assuming your 3.4 is stock. Is the increased power/torque sort of noticeable or very noticeable over that of a 2.8?||||reg||1
Z||000005||gangrenefiero||09-29-2001||04:46 PM||||So the 3.4 from the Grand Ams will use the same computer and wiring? but it has so many different sensors? also i wanted to put in the other transmission to have that extra gear, three speed sucks gas on long trips. And Yes they did there own engine transplants but they are body shop men so they have all the tools and know how. But the thing about the 120+ and burning the tires is true, when do i need that much power. Really i just want to replace the 2.8 cuz it has 145+ on it and i dont know how much longer it will last. But i found a 3.4 with only 1700 miles on it out of a wrecked GA and it comes with the cradle trans and computer, harness and anything else i might need for under 1200obo so he will probably go 900 or so.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Spektrum-87GT||09-29-2001||09:48 PM||||from what i've heard... the 2 swaps are similar, more info at <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A><P>i dont think it uses the same computer, you have to 'piggyback' them.<p>[This message has been edited by Spektrum-87GT (edited 09-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Old Lar||09-29-2001||11:57 PM||||Westtexas, The 3.4 crate is stock.  My plan was to have a highly reliable engine, since I mostly outgrew street racing. I also had a T440 autmatic put in, so I have a hard time comparing it to the prvious 3.1L with the t125 automatic.  The 3.1L/t125 felt real quick with lots of power between 40-70 mph.<BR>The 3.4L/T440 also feels real quick between 40-90 mph. Hard acceleraton from a comlete stop is exhilerating with a hard second gear shift a 5000 rpm, all in a very short time.  I don't do that type of acceleration very often, and I have no idea how fast I'm going. The car will stay in second to at least 65 mph and at least 120 in 3rd.  Top end is a question mark. At 100 mph in 4th OD the engine is turning 2500 rpm.  I have too many concerns driving that fast, so don't get up past 80 too many times.  Maybe when I take it down to Daytona in the spring next year I'll find out.  I95 is a drag strip from the Carolinas to Floida.<P>What I like is the 30-32 mpg I'm getting over the road, using cruise at 72-75 mph.<P>My 87 has a 2.8 and a 5 speed an 80 is very attainable in third at 5000 rpm.||||reg||1
Z||000008||DRH||09-30-2001||12:17 AM||||The old 3.4 RWD from a Camaro can be used with the Fiero intake and electronics. The newer Grand Ams have a <B>3400</B>. It's a whole different animal and I doubt it would run with a Fiero computer.<P>If you swap a 3400 complete with tranny and ECM it should be roughly the same work as doing a 3800 (N/A or SC) also using it's tranny and ECM. There will be some different problems, but it should pretty much balance out. You *might* be able to run factory exhaust manifolds (not sure if they will bolt up) with a 3400. It could save some trouble/money over the 3800 which will definitely need custom exhaust. But it might not have enough flow for the 3400 anyway...<P>The 3400 is a 175HP, 205 ft/lbs, 60 degree V6 with aluminum heads. The 3800 N/A is a 205HP, 230 ft/lbs, 90 degree V6. The 3800 SC is 240HP, 280 ft/lbs.<P>I guess it's a question of whether or not another 30HP or 75HP is worth it to you.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||gangrenefiero||09-30-2001||11:48 AM||||But a 3.4 from a camaro, since its not mounted sideways, arent all the accessories in the wrong places and would call for either using the old fiero stuff or to modify the engine to fit? Also is there a automatic transaxle that will bolt up to that 3.4 besides the Fiero Auto. I like that gas mileage with the OD. And every one is telling me to go to cwestco.com and all i see there about the 3.4 is the DOHC conversion and nothing about the 3400. I dont know, i guess finding either a 3400 or a 3800 is about the same, the hard part is getting the wiring harness completed. I remember Earl, Lil Devil, had his car down for almost a year waiting for this wiring harness and putting it all back together. ANd since they live 500 miles away, i cant have them physically help me on my conversion. UGH is so confusing, i now there are 3 possible ways to go.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Old Lar||09-30-2001||12:37 PM||||The 3.4 Camaro engine needs the starter moved.  The Fiero components bolt right on.  If you are unsure of doing the job yourself (as in my case), Twin Lakes Fiero in Illinois, Bob Steiger or The Fiero Facory in Alabama, Ed Parks have done many and are close to the Carolinas as opposed to Chris West who is in California.  You would be looking at $4K or so using a new crate engine. Less for a used engine. If your heart is set on a 3.4 DOHC, see Chris.  Let your wallet be your guide.<P>As or 3800 swaps, Twin Lakes does them as does the Fiero Factory in Alabama, the Fiero Factory in Toronto as does ACE in Colorado.  Lot of choices.||||reg||1
Z||000011||pontiacs4me||09-30-2001||06:18 PM||||personally I prefer the L67 3800 series II supercharged v6.  I will be beginning my swap soon.  I should be picking up my engine by late next week.  Within 2 weeks I will have my website up and running, within this website I will detail everything necessary to complete this swap, from mounts to wiring to axles, even going into the engine to do mods.  I will be porting the heads and intake.  Installing a custom supercharger cooling system, installing new engine bearings and piston rings, thermal coating the pistons for nitrous, installing a smaller pulley, polishing the blower, and painting everything orange and silver.  Oh yeah one more thing building custom headers and exhaust system.  Im not releasing the name of the website yet, until its up and running, but this site will be a major contribution to the fiero community.  Ill definately keep everyone posted as to the location of this website as soon as it is working.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Joe Torma||09-30-2001||06:39 PM||||I have a 3400 in my car.  Awesome motor.  Lighter than stock, roller lifters and rockers stock.  Desktop dyno says I have about 300hp with my mods.  You need to make a front mount and do something with the high mount alternator.  I made a vent/scoop on the lid and relocated the dogbone because it sits too high.  I think Steve Normington moved his alt.<P>Using the stock 2.8 computer is out of the question.  Keeping the stock 3400 injectors and intake, I used a TECII computer system, Steve used stock 3400 computer.<P>I also made custom hedders. took my 6months to engineer.<P>There are other ways to do the 3400. I think you can see one from the FieroSails links page.<P>The 3800 is nice setup, but I don't know much about it.<P>EDIT...I think faster than I type<BR>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig1.gif">     <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 10-01-2001).]||||reg||1
